<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="nitric oxide biosynthesis II (mammals)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4983" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: NITRIC-OXIDE Nitric oxide| (NO) plays an important role in cell signaling in the nervous system, and is implicated in synaptic plasticity. It also participates in the immunologic response to infection. In the vasculature NO relaxes smooth muscle and inhibits platelet and leukocyte adhesion. NO is synthesized from arginine by nitric oxide synthase (NOS), generating |FRAME:L-CITRULLINE| as a by-product. NO production is absolutely dependent on arginine availability. Arginine can be provided either by exogenous sources (via blood circulation) or by the citrulline-NO cycle. In this cycle, arginine is used by NOS forming NO and citrulline, and then regenerated from citrulline by the combined action of the enzymes |FRAME:CPLX-6641| and |FRAME:CPLX-6661|. The citrulline-NO cycle functions in many different types of cells. It has been demonstrated in endothelial cells |CITS:[2236071]|, macrophages |CITS:[1731766]|, and the peripheral nervous system, including canine enteric neurons |CITS:[8545001]|, opossum internal anal sphincter |CITS:[9098009]| and canine esophageal sphincter |CITS:[9575812]|. The different enzymes of this cycle are coregulated. Coregulation of |FRAME:CPLX-6582| and |FRAME:CPLX-6641| has been shown in activated murine macrophage-like cells |CITS:[7507106]|, in vascular smooth muscle cells |CITS:[7511585]|, in in vivo-activated rat and mouse macrophages |CITS:[8576237][10409134]|, and in rodent and human pancreatic &amp;beta;-cells |CITS:[7628352]|. In addition, a coinduction of |FRAME:CPLX-6582|, |FRAME:CPLX-6641| and |FRAME:CPLX-6661| has been demonstrated PC12 cells |CITS:[11072090]|.</Description>
